{
  "headline": "Fine-tuning scGPT on the CRISPRi screen it is evaluated on, with scGPT's own published perturbation recipe, leaves the queried response features inside the shuffled-label band and far below the model's own static gene table. Across the 10 fine-tuned models (five gene-table arms in two cell lines), every one leaves them inside the band, and the highest any reaches is 0.0456, against the gene table at 0.2279 in HepG2 and 0.2318 in Jurkat. Paired against the frozen model's own response features, 8 of the 10 differences include zero; the exceptions are Jurkat ESM2-3B table, trainable against the frozen arm, 0.0313; HepG2 ESM2-3B table, frozen against the frozen arm, -0.01222, a few hundredths of a correlation point and in opposite directions. One secondary read-out is not a clean null: querying the checkpoints through the model's own perturbation-flag input rather than through the value substitution runs 2 and 3 use puts 2 of 10 readings above both the band and that query's own shuffled draws (Jurkat scGPT table, trainable 0.0565; Jurkat ESM2-3B table, frozen 0.0751), and those two are reported as unexplained rather than resolved. On the model's own prediction head the predicted profile matches the measured effect at 0.5451 in HepG2 and 0.3447 in Jurkat, but that agreement is not perturbation-specific: measuring only what the perturbation flag changes gives 0.0818 and 0.0020, and removing the held-out fold's own mean from both sides gives -0.0338 and 0.0190. Only 0.1202 and 0.0552 of a predicted profile's variance survives subtracting the mean prediction, against 0.7444 and 0.8694 for the measurement. What fine-tuning installed is close to one profile for every knockdown, so a read-out that asks which gene was knocked down has very little to recover.",
  "assessment": "The pre-registered prediction holds in its first half: the response features stay inside the shuffled-label band in all ten fine-tuned models, and the gene-table arm stays 5 to 43 times higher. Its second half, that the model might predict better even so, is not borne out either, but the evidence for that is of two different strengths and they should not be run together.\n\nWhat the data supports. No fine-tuned arm's response features clear the band. The paired differences against the frozen model's own response features are small; most include zero, and the 2 that do not (Jurkat ESM2-3B table, trainable against the frozen arm, 0.0313; HepG2 ESM2-3B table, frozen against the frozen arm, -0.01222) are a few hundredths of a correlation point, point in opposite directions, and sit among the 32 paired differences computed on this page, so neither is evidence on its own. The model's predicted profile matches the measured effect when the reference is the measured control, and stops matching when the reference is the model's own unflagged output, which locates that agreement in a systematic offset shared across perturbations rather than in a response to the gene. The strongest single statement is the spread of the prediction itself, which is not a correlation and carries no interval width: almost none of a predicted profile's variance survives subtracting the mean prediction, and the profiles predicted for two different knockdowns correlate above 0.9.\n\nWhat it does not support. This is not a demonstration that fine-tuning cannot install the signal. The fold-centred correlation intervals are wide enough to admit a modest positive effect, and on the restricted folds co-expression itself is not separated from the band, so the evaluation cannot resolve a co-expression-sized effect there. The result is a bound, not a zero: at the resolution these folds achieve, fine-tuning produces no detectable improvement in the queried response features and leaves them far below the gene table. One secondary read-out is not a clean null: querying the checkpoints through the model's own perturbation-flag input rather than through the value substitution runs 2 and 3 use puts 2 of 10 readings above both the band and that query's own shuffled draws (Jurkat scGPT table, trainable 0.0565; Jurkat ESM2-3B table, frozen 0.0751), and those two are reported as unexplained rather than resolved. That column's own shuffled draws are several times wider than the band and uneven across arms, the other arms in the same cell line do not reproduce it, and both readings stay three to four times below the gene table, so it is reported as a noisy reading rather than as a signal.\n\nScope and limits. One architecture and one recipe, run on the screen the question is about, which is the setting most favourable to installing the signal and therefore the setting in which a null is most informative; it does not speak to other architectures or to fine-tuning on a different screen. A fine-tuned model may only be scored on perturbations it never saw, so the main arms read two of run 3's five folds: in HepG2 the shuffled-label draws reach 0.0550 and co-expression itself scores 0.0072 on the 54 response groups these folds leave; in Jurkat the shuffled-label draws reach 0.0555 and co-expression itself scores 0.0259 on the 137 response groups these folds leave. Four controls address the obvious objections: the arm is refitted on the complementary folds so the pooled result covers the whole screen, the batch sampler is run in the exact upstream configuration as well as ours, the alternative checkpoint-selection rule is tracked inside every run and read again in a separate earlier execution of all sixteen arms, and every recipe setting including the selection rule is checked field by field against the published notebook.",
  "decision_rule": {
    "preregistered": "run 3's rule, unchanged: residual target, reliability-weighted, ceiling-corrected per-perturbation Pearson correlation, median over held-out perturbations of the grouped split, read against the shuffled-label band and against the same model's static gene-table arm",
    "restriction_added_by_this_run": "only folds 0 and 1 are scored, because a fine-tuned model must not be scored on a perturbation it trained on. The validation split used for epoch selection is drawn from the TRAINING folds, never from these",
    "reported_beside_it": "the fold-centred version of the same correlation, and the model's own predicted response scored with no translator in the path",
    "not_substituted": "the verdict is read from the pre-registered cell. The direct-prediction result is additional evidence about the model, not a replacement metric"
  },
  "methods": {
    "base_model": "wanglab/scGPT-human at a24c237737a40f3720f75abb555489e9fe753be6",
    "recipe": "scGPT's published perturbation tutorial: masked_mse_loss(all positions), Adam at lr 0.0001, batch 64, up to 15 epochs with early stop after 10, schedule gamma 0.9; selection on validation Pearson over held-out perturbations (the tutorial's val_metrics[\"pearson\"]); masked MSE recorded and selected on in parallel",
    "arms": {
      "own_trainable": "the standard published recipe",
      "own_frozen": "gain attributable to the readout alone",
      "esm2_trainable": "protein provenance as the starting table",
      "esm2_frozen": "the same, with the table held fixed",
      "rand_frozen": "capacity-matched null for the two ESM2 arms: the same width and the same learned projection, over vectors that carry no information about the gene"
    },
    "heldout_folds": [
      0,
      1
    ],
    "feature_queries": "run 2's value-substitution family, unmodified, so the fine-tuned numbers are commensurable with the frozen ones; plus the model's own perturbation-flag query, which is the query the fine-tuning objective is defined on",
    "restatement_checks": "the masked-MSE loss is compared numerically against the installed scgpt/loss.py at load time and the result recorded in each run's provenance.json; --upstream-sampling reproduces the published batch sampler exactly, including the ~40% of rows that lose their perturbed gene",
    "n_training_runs": 16,
    "checkpoint_selection": "the tutorial's own rule, read out of its training-loop cell by src/scfm_ft/recipe_provenance.py: keep the epoch with the highest mean Pearson correlation, across all genes, between each held-out perturbation's measured and predicted mean expression profile, and apply the patience counter to that score. The masked MSE on the same held-out cells is tracked in parallel with its own patience counter, so the epoch that rule would keep is recorded from the same run and both checkpoints are saved",
    "selection_rule_sensitivity": "the two rules keep the same epoch in 6 of 16 runs of this execution, where the selected weights are therefore identical; separately, an earlier execution of the same sixteen arms selected on masked MSE throughout has 10 arms comparable here and 10 of them are inside the shuffled-label band in both executions. That earlier execution predates the three faithfulness controls, so it differs in random initialisation as well as in the rule and is read as a replicate"
  },
